            <ArticleTitle>Anti-immunoglobulin e in the treatment of refractory atopic dermatitis.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>554-8</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1097/SMJ.0b013e3181de0cf6</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Atopic dermatitis (AD) is a common diagnosis seen in both children and adults, and it is often the first manifestation of atopic disease. Research has shown a strong correlation between serum IgE levels, the severity of atopic dermatitis, and co-existing asthma and/or allergic rhinitis. Omalizumab (Xolair, East Hanover, NJ; Genentech, South San Francisco, CA) is a monoclonal antibody to human IgE and is currently Food and Drug Administration (FDA) approved for the treatment of asthma. We present 3 patients with severe, treatment resistant atopic dermatitis whose cutaneous symptoms significantly improved by treatment with omalizumab.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
